      "id": "n5-u02-g05",
      "title": "ね・よ — \"right?\" and \"you know\"",
      "pattern": "〜ですね / 〜ですよ",
      "meaning": "…, isn't it? / …, you know!",
      "explanation": "Japanese adds small words to the end of a sentence to show feeling. ね asks the listener to agree or confirm, like \"…, right?\" or \"…, isn't it?\". よ tells the listener something they don't know yet, like \"…, you know!\" or \"Hey, …!\". そうですね means \"That's right\" or \"Let me see…\".",
      "notes": [
        "Use ね when you think the listener already knows or agrees.",
        "Use よ to give new information; using it too much can sound pushy."
      ],
